WebKitGTK: show the pointer-with-hourglass while a page loads.

A page that is still loading, or whose web process has stopped answering,
looked exactly like one that was ready: the arrow, a page that does not yet
scroll, and no cue but the progress number in luakit's status bar.  The
cursor theme generated into /usr/share/icons/LikeOS has had the "progress"
cursor (the pointer with the small hourglass beside it) since the theme was
added; nothing ever asked for it.

Drive it from the two states the view already tracks.  WebKitWebView's
is-loading (WEBKIT_LOAD_STARTED until FINISHED or the failure that ends the
load) and is-web-process-responsive (the responsiveness timer, which trips
when a click or key press goes unanswered for three seconds) feed one "busy"
flag on the GTK widget.  While it is set the widget's window carries the
progress cursor; the cursors WebCore keeps sending for whatever is under the
pointer are held back and the last of them is put in place when the wait
ends, or, with none, whatever was shown before.  A load that starts before
the widget is realized shows the cursor from realize().

Wheel events are not a signal here: on this port they go to the web process's
event dispatcher without a reply, so nothing in the UI process can tell a
scroll that was handled from one that is waiting.

